(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-1-anatomy-master-exam-form-b"]={"title":"Anatomy Comprehensive Master Exam — Form B","category":"Anatomy","questions":[{"q":"What do \"supine\" and \"prone\" describe?","choices":["Supine is lying on the back; prone is lying face down on the ventral surface","Supine describes the hand's palm; prone describes the foot's sole","Supine describes lying face down; prone describes lying on one's back","Supine and prone are both terms used to describe upright standing postures"],"answer":0,"exp":"Supine means lying on the back, while prone means lying on the ventral surface (face down). — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What does \"rostral\" mean, and in what context is it often used?","choices":["Situated toward the bottom end of the body, a term often applied when describing the limbs","Situated at the body's midline, used only in embryology and development","Situated near the front of the body (Latin for \"beak\"), often used in neurological terms","Situated on the lateral surface, used in orthopedics and joint description"],"answer":2,"exp":"Rostral means situated near the front of the body (Latin for \"beak\"), sometimes used interchangeably with anterior, and is often used in neurological terms. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What does \"caudal\" mean, and in what context is it often used?","choices":["Situated near the bottom or end of an organism (Latin for \"tail\"), often used in neurological/spinal terms","Situated superiorly, used exclusively in skull and cranial terminology","Situated toward the front of the body, sometimes used interchangeably with the term anterior","Situated on the medial surface, used specifically for the hand and wrist"],"answer":0,"exp":"Caudal means situated near the bottom or end of an organism (Latin for \"tail\"), sometimes used interchangeably with posterior, and is often used in neurological terms related to the spine. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What does \"cephalic\" mean?","choices":["Toward the head (Greek for \"head\"), sometimes used interchangeably with superior","Directed toward the back surface of the body, in the dorsal direction","Toward the midline of the body, from the Latin for \"middle\"","Directed toward the feet, a term sometimes used interchangeably with inferior"],"answer":0,"exp":"Cephalic means toward the head (Greek for \"head\"), sometimes used interchangeably with superior, and is often used in neurology. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What does \"ventral\" denote, and when is it typically used?","choices":["Superior to another structure; typically used when describing skull sutures and cranial landmarks specifically","Medial to another structure; typically used when describing midline trunk structures specifically","Anterior to another structure; typically used when a structure divides into two parts with an opposite dorsal structure (e.g., ventral horn vs. dorsal horn)","Posterior to another structure; typically used when describing paired limb landmarks specifically"],"answer":2,"exp":"Ventral denotes something anterior to another structure, typically used when the anatomical structure is easily divided into two parts with an opposite dorsal structure, such as the ventral horn versus dorsal horn. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What does \"palmar\" describe?","choices":["The posterior surface of the hand","The superior surface of a protruding body part","The anterior surface of the hand","The inferior surface of the foot"],"answer":2,"exp":"Palmar is used to describe the \"anterior\" surface of the hand. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What does \"plantar\" describe?","choices":["The posterior surface of the hand, called the dorsum","The inferior surface of the foot, not visible when standing","The anterior surface of the hand, called the palm","The superior surface of the foot, the dorsum"],"answer":1,"exp":"Plantar describes the \"inferior\" surface of the foot — the surface that is not visible when standing. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What does \"dorsal\" describe when referring to a protruding body part, and what are some examples given?","choices":["The anterior surface of the hand only, called the palmar surface of the palm","The superior/back surface of a part that protrudes anterior from the body — e.g., dorsum of the foot, hand, penis, or tongue","The medial surface of the trunk, located close to the body's vertical midline","The inferior surface of the foot only, called the plantar surface of the sole"],"answer":1,"exp":"Dorsal describes the superior or dorsal (back) surface of any part that protrudes anterior from the body, with examples including the dorsum of the foot, the dorsum of the hand, the dorsum of the penis, and the dorsum of the tongue. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What does \"inferomedial\" mean, and what is the example given?","choices":["Situated nearer to the head and farther away from the median plane, as with the superolateral brain surface","Nearer to the feet and farther from the median plane; e.g., the inferolateral rib margin","Nearer to the feet and closer to the median plane; e.g., the anterior ribs are directed inferomedially","Nearer to the head and closer to the median plane; e.g., the superomedial orbit"],"answer":2,"exp":"Inferomedial means nearer to the feet and closer to the median plane; the example given is that the anterior parts of the ribs are directed inferomedially. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What is the difference between \"bilateral\" and \"unilateral\" structures, with examples?","choices":["Paired structures (kidneys, lungs, extremities) are bilateral; single-sided structures (spleen, liver) are unilateral","Bilateral is used to describe structures present on only one side; unilateral describes paired structures","Both terms are said to describe the exact same thing: structures present on both sides","Bilateral and unilateral both refer only to structures of the upper and lower limbs"],"answer":0,"exp":"Paired structures such as the kidneys, lungs, and extremities are bilateral, left, or right, while structures found on only one side, such as the spleen and liver, are unilateral. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Where is transitional epithelium found, and what is distinctive about its surface cells?","choices":["Cube-shaped cells at the surface; found lining the collecting ducts of the kidney","Column-shaped cells at the surface; found lining the wall of the digestive tract","Flat surface cells; found lining the thin walls of capillaries and alveoli","Dome-shaped surface cells; found lining the bladder, ureters, and superior urethra"],"answer":3,"exp":"Transitional epithelium has dome-shaped surface cells and is found lining the bladder, ureters, and superior urethra. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What are the three general categories of \"living\" cells found in connective tissue, based on suffix naming?","choices":["\"Blast\" cells (remove), \"cyte\" cells (create), and \"clast\" cells (maintain)","Macrophages, neurons, and chondrocytes","\"Blast\" cells (create), \"cyte\" cells (maintain), and \"clast\" cells (remove)","Only \"cyte\" cells exist in connective tissue"],"answer":2,"exp":"Connective tissue's living cellular components include \"blast\" cells (create), \"cyte\" cells (maintain), and \"clast\" cells (remove), along with macrophages, white blood cells, mast cells, and adipose cells. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which of the following is a listed function of connective tissue?","choices":["Contracts its fibers to generate voluntary body movement","Generates nerve impulses and transmits them along its length","Connects, binds, and supports structures (e.g., tendons, ligaments)","Filters and secretes materials as a lining"],"answer":2,"exp":"Connective tissue connects, binds, and supports structures such as tendons and ligaments, in addition to protecting/cushioning organs, insulating, and transporting substances like blood. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What is the \"matrix\" of connective tissue, and what are its three components?","choices":["The nonliving component, consisting of ground substance, fluid, and fibers","A structure unique to bone tissue only, absent from all other connective tissue types","A membrane-like structure found exclusively within epithelial tissue, not connective tissue","The living cellular portion of connective tissue, made up of blast, cyte, and clast cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"The matrix is the \"non-living\" component of connective tissue, consisting of ground substance (proteoglycan aggregates, glycosaminoglycans, hyaluronic acid), fluid (containing gases and nutrients, plus minerals), and fibers (collagen, elastin, and reticular fibers). — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What is collagen, and what is a notable property of it?","choices":["A form of ground substance that lacks any meaningful structural strength","A mineral component of the connective tissue matrix, similar to calcium salts","A specialized cell type found only within the matrix of bone tissue","A fibrous protein constituting about 25% of the weight of most mammals, with high tensile strength"],"answer":3,"exp":"Collagen is a fibrous protein present in connective tissue structure (derived from a Greek word meaning \"to glue together\"), constituting about 25% of the weight of most mammals, and has high tensile strength (a 4.5 pound load is needed to break a collagen fiber 1 mm thick). — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What are reticular fibers, structurally?","choices":["Mineral deposits found specifically within the calcified matrix of bone","Thick elastin fibers with pronounced spring-like recoil properties","Very fine collagen fibers that branch out to form a network, filling space between tissues and organs","Cellular structures found uniquely within epithelial tissue linings, not connective tissue"],"answer":2,"exp":"Reticular fibers are actually very fine collagen fibers that branch out to form a network, filling the space between other tissues and organs. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What are elastic fibers, and what property do they have?","choices":["Rigid mineral crystals embedded permanently within the bone matrix","Fibers containing the protein elastin, with spring-like properties that can be compressed or distended","Fine collagen fibers forming a delicate, loosely branching network","Cellular fibers found only within the fascicles of muscle tissue"],"answer":1,"exp":"Elastic fibers contain the protein elastin and have spring-like properties, able to be compressed or distended. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What is areolar (\"loose connective\") tissue, and what is one of its functions?","choices":["A mineral-rich tissue found only within the calcified matrix of compact bone","A dense, tightly packed fiber tissue found only within tendons and ligaments","An avascular tissue type found only within the matrix of hyaline cartilage structures","Serves as \"packing material\" with elastic and collagen fibers, and separates muscles to allow them to slide over each other"],"answer":3,"exp":"Areolar tissue is a loose connective tissue serving as \"packing material\" with elastic and collagen fibers; it forms a basal membrane component and separates muscles, allowing them to slide over each other. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What is distinctive about adipose tissue's cellular composition, and what are its functions?","choices":["Cells account for only 10% of tissue mass, mostly matrix; functions primarily in mineral storage","Cells account for 90% of tissue mass (little matrix present), engorged with lipids; functions as insulator, shock absorber, and energy store","Consists mostly of collagen fibers with few cells; functions primarily in blood cell production","Highly avascular tissue with a notably slow healing rate; its main function is body movement"],"answer":1,"exp":"Adipose tissue is highly vascular, with cells accounting for 90% of tissue mass (little matrix present) and engorged with lipids; it functions as an insulator, shock absorber, and energy store. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What distinguishes dense regular connective tissue from dense irregular connective tissue?","choices":["Both describe the identical parallel fiber arrangement seen only in tendons, with no meaningful structural difference between the two categories at all","Dense regular is found only in cartilage matrix; dense irregular is found only in compact bone tissue, with each restricted entirely to that one tissue type","Dense regular has closely packed parallel collagen fibers (tendons, ligaments) where tension runs one direction; dense irregular has closely packed non-directional collagen forming sheets (dermis, fascia) where tension runs many directions","Dense regular has non-directional collagen forming sheets (dermis, fascia) where tension runs many directions; dense irregular has parallel collagen fibers (tendons, ligaments) where tension runs one direction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Dense regular connective tissue has closely packed parallel collagen fibers, found where tension is exerted in a particular direction (tendons, ligaments), while dense irregular connective tissue has closely packed non-directional collagen forming sheets where tension is exerted in many directions (dermis of skin, muscle fascia, organ and nerve coverings). — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What are association fibers, commissural fibers, and projection fibers?","choices":["Association fibers connect the two hemispheres directly; commissural fibers interconnect areas within a single hemisphere; projection fibers are functionally identical to association fibers","All three fiber types actually describe the exact same underlying white matter tracts, simply using different, entirely interchangeable historical naming conventions","Association fibers interconnect areas within a hemisphere; commissural fibers connect the two hemispheres; projection fibers link the cerebrum with other CNS parts","Only association fibers actually exist within the cerebrum at all; both commissural and projection fibers are instead found only within the spinal cord"],"answer":2,"exp":"Association fibers are tracts that interconnect areas of neural cortex within a hemisphere (e.g., arcuate fibers), commissural fibers connect the two hemispheres (e.g., corpus callosum), and projection fibers link the cerebrum with other parts of the brain and spinal cord. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What are the basal nuclei, and what structures make them up?","choices":["Masses of white matter tracts located superior to the lateral ventricles, connecting the thalamus and hypothalamus directly to one another throughout the diencephalon","Part of the cerebellum entirely rather than the cerebrum, consisting specifically of the dentate, fastigial, and interposed cerebellar nuclei themselves","A single nucleus located deep within the occipital lobe, responsible solely for conscious visual processing and spatial depth perception overall","Masses of gray matter embedded in the white matter inferior to the lateral ventricles, consisting of the caudate nucleus, amygdaloid body, claustrum, putamen, and globus pallidus"],"answer":3,"exp":"The basal nuclei are masses of gray matter embedded in the white matter inferior to the lateral ventricles, consisting of the caudate nucleus, amygdaloid body, claustrum, putamen, and globus pallidus. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What is the amygdaloid body (amygdala), and what is it essential for?","choices":["A component of the limbic system located in the temporal lobe, essential for processing emotional reactions","A structure processing visual information, located deep within the occipital lobe itself","Part of the brainstem entirely, essential specifically for regulating heart rate and respiratory rate","A structure controlling walking and gait movement patterns, located within the parietal lobe itself"],"answer":0,"exp":"The amygdaloid body (amygdala) is a component of the limbic system located in the temporal lobe, essential for processing emotional reactions. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Where is the limbic system located, and what are its functions?","choices":["Within the medulla oblongata specifically, near the base of the brain stem itself; regulates breathing rate and cardiovascular reflexes automatically","Within the cerebellum only, inferior to the occipital lobe entirely; coordinates fine motor function, balance, and posture on an ongoing basis","Within the occipital lobe specifically, posterior to the parietal lobe entirely; processes visual stimuli and spatial depth perception exclusively","Between the cerebrum and diencephalon, superior to the corpus callosum; establishes emotional states, links conscious/unconscious functions, and facilitates memory"],"answer":3,"exp":"The limbic system is located between the cerebrum and the diencephalon, just superior to the corpus callosum, and functions to establish emotional states, link conscious functions with unconscious autonomic functions, and facilitate memory storage and retrieval. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What is the fornix, and what do the mamillary bodies control?","choices":["A structure located deep within the brainstem itself; mamillary bodies instead control auditory reflex responses to sudden loud sound","A white matter tract connecting the hippocampus to the hypothalamus; mamillary bodies control reflex movements associated with eating","A tract connecting the amygdala directly to the thalamus; mamillary bodies instead control visual tracking reflex movements","A gray matter mass connecting the cerebellum directly to the medulla; mamillary bodies instead control respiratory breathing rate"],"answer":1,"exp":"The fornix is a tract of white matter connecting the hippocampus to the hypothalamus, with many fornix fibers extending to the mamillary bodies, which control reflex movements associated with eating. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"How many ventricles are there, and where is the 4th ventricle located?","choices":["Four ventricles; the 4th ventricle is located between the pons and cerebellum","Four ventricles; the 4th ventricle is located within the temporal lobe itself","Two ventricles only; the so-called '4th ventricle' does not actually exist","Three ventricles total; the 4th ventricle sits within the diencephalon itself"],"answer":0,"exp":"There are four ventricles: two lateral ventricles in the cerebral hemispheres, a 3rd ventricle in the diencephalon, and a 4th ventricle located between the pons and cerebellum. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What separates the two lateral ventricles in the cerebral hemispheres?","choices":["The corpus callosum","The tentorium cerebelli","The falx cerebri","The septum pellucidum"],"answer":3,"exp":"The septum pellucidum is the partition that separates the two lateral ventricles in the cerebral hemispheres. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What does the epithalamus contain, and to what is it attached?","choices":["Contains the hypothalamus entirely, attached to the pituitary gland below","Contains the amygdala instead, attached directly to the hippocampus","Contains the pineal gland, attached to the roof of the third ventricle","Contains the thalamus instead, attached to the corpus callosum above"],"answer":2,"exp":"The epithalamus contains the pineal gland, which is attached to the roof of the third ventricle. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What does the thalamus do, and how many thalami are there?","choices":["Controls breathing and heart rate directly all by itself; there is only a single, midline thalamus present","Regulates emotions and thirst directly by itself; there is only a single, unpaired thalamus present","Produces melatonin directly on its own, like the pineal gland; there is a right and left thalamus","Relays information to the cerebrum and processes sensory information; there is a right and left thalamus"],"answer":3,"exp":"The thalamus relays information to the cerebrum and processes sensory information, forming the walls of the 3rd ventricle; there is a right and a left thalamus. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What is the hypothalamus involved in, per the material?","choices":["Emotions, thirst, and some habitual activity","Producing the pineal hormone melatonin","Auditory and visual processing","Relaying sensory information to the cerebrum only"],"answer":0,"exp":"The hypothalamus is involved in emotions, thirst, and some habitual activity. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What is the axon hillock?","choices":["Bundles of neurofilaments within the axoplasm","Clusters of free ribosomes within the cell body","The cytoplasm contained within the axon","The area where the initial segment connects to the cell body"],"answer":3,"exp":"The axon hillock is the area where the initial segment connects to the cell body. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What are Nissl bodies?","choices":["The specific region joining the axon to the neuron's cell body","The cytoskeleton framework of the neuron cell","The distal ends of the axon together with its branching collaterals","Clusters of free ribosomes (and rough ER) within the neuron"],"answer":3,"exp":"Nissl bodies are clusters of free ribosomes (clusters of rough endoplasmic reticulum and free ribosomes). — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What are telodendria and axon terminals?","choices":["Telodendria are described as clusters of ribosomes; axon terminals are described as bundles of neurofilaments","Telodendria are the branches that arise off the axon; axon terminals are described as the cytoplasm within the axon","Telodendria are the ends of the axon and its collaterals; axon terminals are the ends of the telodendria","Telodendria and axon terminals are alternate names for the cell body itself"],"answer":2,"exp":"Telodendria are the ends of the axon and its collaterals, and axon terminals are the ends of the telodendria. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What are astrocytes, and what do they form?","choices":["The largest and most numerous CNS glial cells; make up the blood-brain barrier","Cells that line the fluid-filled passageways of the brain and play a role in CSF production","Cells responsible for forming the myelin sheath around axons of the central nervous system","Phagocytic cells that migrate through tissue and remove cellular waste products"],"answer":0,"exp":"Astrocytes are the largest and most numerous CNS neuroglia, and they make up the blood-brain barrier (BBB). — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What do oligodendrocytes do, and what is the difference between white and gray matter?","choices":["Oligodendrocytes produce CSF within the ventricles; white and gray matter are functionally the same tissue","Oligodendrocytes form the myelin sheath and internodes; areas with mostly myelinated axons are white matter, areas devoid of myelinated axons are gray matter","Oligodendrocytes are classified as phagocytic cells; white matter is said to contain cell bodies while gray matter contains myelinated axons","Oligodendrocytes form the blood-brain barrier at capillaries; white matter is entirely devoid of axons"],"answer":1,"exp":"Oligodendrocytes form the myelin sheath and myelin sheath gaps (internodes); areas with mostly myelinated axons are called white matter, while areas devoid of myelinated axons (composed of cell bodies and dendrites) are called gray matter. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What are microglia, and what is their function?","choices":["Cells that produce myelin sheaths wrapped tightly around CNS axons","Star-shaped glial cells responsible for forming the blood-brain barrier","Phagocytic cells that remove unwanted substances as they move through nervous tissue","Cuboidal epithelial-type cells that line the fluid-filled passageways of the CNS"],"answer":2,"exp":"Microglia are phagocytic cells that move through nervous tissue, removing unwanted substances. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What are ependymal cells, and what do they form?","choices":["Phagocytic cells that remove waste from nervous tissue while migrating throughout the entire CNS","Simple cuboidal epithelial cells lining fluid-filled passageways, involved in CSF production; they make up a cellular lining called the ependyma","Cells that form the myelin sheath around CNS axons, wrapping their membrane repeatedly around each segment","Star-shaped cells anchoring themselves to capillaries, together forming the blood-brain barrier structure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Ependymal cells are simple cuboidal epithelial cells that line fluid-filled passageways within the brain and spinal cord, are involved in CSF production, and make up a cellular lining called the ependyma. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What are the two types of neuroglia in the PNS, and what does each surround?","choices":["Satellite cells (surround cell bodies) and Schwann cells (surround axons)","The PNS contains a single type of neuroglia: the Schwann cell","Astrocytes, which surround cell bodies, and oligodendrocytes, which surround axons","Microglia, which surround cell bodies, and ependymal cells, which surround axons"],"answer":0,"exp":"There are two types of neuroglia in the PNS: satellite cells, which surround cell bodies, and Schwann cells, which surround axons. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What is another name for Schwann cells, and what do they consist of?","choices":["Also called ependymal cells; consist of cilia lining passageways","Also called satellite cells; consist of the perikaryon region","Also referred to as astrocytes; composed solely of the myelin sheath itself","Also called neurolemmocytes; consist of the axolemma/neurolemma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Schwann cells are also called neurolemmocytes, and they consist of the axolemma/neurolemma. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What is an internode, and what are nodes (of Ranvier)?","choices":["Internodes and nodes only exist within the CNS structures, never appearing in the PNS at all","Both terms are said to describe the exact same portion of the myelinated peripheral axon segment","An internode is the portion of a myelinated axon between two successive Schwann cells; nodes are the small gaps separating internodes","An internode refers to the small gap located between adjacent Schwann cells; nodes refer to the myelinated segments themselves"],"answer":2,"exp":"An internode is the portion of a myelinated nerve axon between two successive Schwann cells, and nodes (or nodes of Ranvier) are the small gaps that separate internodes. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"In the dorsal column sensory arrangement, how are the gracile and cuneate fasciculi organized medially/laterally, and what does each represent?","choices":["Gracilis is more lateral, responsible only for trunk sensation; cuneatus is more medial, responsible only for pelvic girdle sensation","Gracilis is more lateral, responsible for the upper extremity; cuneatus is more medial, responsible for the lower extremity and trunk","Both fasciculi occupy the identical medial-lateral position, together representing only trunk and axial body sensation","Gracilis is more medial, responsible for the lower extremity; cuneatus is more lateral, responsible for the upper extremity and trunk"],"answer":3,"exp":"The dorsal column sensory material is arranged with the gracilis fasciculus more medial, responsible for the lower extremity, and the cuneatus fasciculus more lateral, responsible for the upper extremity and trunk (ceasing around T5-T6). — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What arteries supply the spinal cord along its length, and what supplements them?","choices":["Only the basilar artery, arising from the union of the two vertebral arteries at the level of the pons, with no segmental supply","A single anterior spinal artery and paired posterior spinal arteries (from the vertebral arteries), supplemented by segmental branches mainly from the aorta","The pulmonary arteries, supplemented segmentally by branches of the internal jugular vein","Only the internal carotid arteries, with no segmental supplementation from the aorta"],"answer":1,"exp":"The vertebral arteries give off a single anterior spinal artery and paired posterior spinal arteries that descend along the cord, supplemented by segmental branches mainly arising from branches of the aorta. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Where do veins of the spinal cord drain, and what venous systems do they ultimately connect to?","choices":["Drain into the pulmonary veins, ultimately connecting directly through to the left atrium of the heart itself","Drain into the hepatic portal vein, ultimately connecting to sinusoids within the liver","Drain directly into the internal jugular vein only, bypassing any vertebral venous plexuses","Drain into vertebral venous plexuses (e.g., internal venous plexus in the epidural space), ultimately connecting to the azygos and caval venous systems"],"answer":3,"exp":"Veins of the spinal cord drain into several vertebral venous plexuses associated with the vertebral column, such as the internal venous plexus within the epidural space, ultimately connecting to the azygos and caval venous systems to reach the heart. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What role do microglia and white blood cells play in spinal cord meningeal lymphatics, per the material?","choices":["They are exclusively involved in CSF production, secreted by the choroid plexus ependymal cells","They play a role in protecting and healing the spinal cord from infections, injury, and immune-mediated disease, often following blood vessel patterning within the meninges","They have no known role in spinal cord immunity, functioning solely as structural support cells","They only function within the peripheral nervous system's Schwann cell sheaths and play no role at all within the spinal cord"],"answer":1,"exp":"Microglia and white blood cells play a role in protecting and healing the spinal cord from infections, injury, and immune-mediated diseases, often following the patterning of blood vessels within the meninges, though understanding of spinal cord meningeal lymphatics and immunity remains limited. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What sensory modalities do the dorsal columns carry?","choices":["Taste, smell, and general visceral sensation","Pain and temperature sensation only, throughout the entire body","Crude touch and pressure sensation only, throughout the body","Vibration, proprioception, and 2-point discrimination"],"answer":3,"exp":"The dorsal columns carry vibration, proprioception, and 2-point discrimination (fine touch). — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What does the fasciculus gracilis carry, and for which body region?","choices":["Fine touch, vibration, 2-point discrimination, and proprioception for the upper body only (T6 and up)","Crude touch and pain sensation only, for the entire body below the level of T7","Fine touch, vibration, 2-point discrimination, and proprioception for the mid-thoracic to feet (T7 and down)","Pain and temperature sensation only, for the lower body below the level of T7"],"answer":2,"exp":"The fasciculus gracilis carries fine touch, vibration, 2-point discrimination, and proprioception for the mid-thoracic to feet region (T7 and inferiorly). — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What does the fasciculus cuneatus carry, and for which body region?","choices":["Fine touch, vibration, and proprioception for the lower extremity only (T7 and down), excluding the trunk and the neck","Crude touch and pain sensation for the face and ears exclusively, bypassing the dorsal columns","Pain and temperature sensation only, for the upper body including the face and ears","Fine touch, vibration, 2-point discrimination, and proprioception for the upper body (T6 and up), including the upper extremity and neck but not the face/ears"],"answer":3,"exp":"The fasciculus cuneatus carries fine touch, vibration, 2-point discrimination, and proprioception for the upper body (T6 and superior), including the upper extremity and neck, but not the face and ears. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Where do the dorsal column tracts decussate, and what do they join after crossing?","choices":["Do not decussate at all","Decussate in the spinal cord itself and join the spinothalamic tract","Decussate in the thalamus and join the corticospinal tract","Decussate in the medulla and join the medial lemniscus"],"answer":3,"exp":"The dorsal column tracts ascend in the spinal cord, decussate in the medulla, and join the medial lemniscus, then enter the ventral nuclei of the thalamus before transmission to the primary sensory cortex. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What do the posterior (dorsal) and anterior (ventral) spinocerebellar tracts have in common?","choices":["Both are motor tracts descending directly from the primary motor cortex down to the lower motor neurons of the anterior horn","Both transmit conscious pain and temperature sensation to the ventral posterior nucleus of the thalamus","Both transmit unconscious proprioceptive information to the cerebellum, receiving input from muscle spindles, Golgi tendon organs, and pressure receptors","Both decussate together in the medulla's pyramids before ascending to the thalamus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Both the posterior and anterior spinocerebellar tracts transmit unconscious proprioceptive information to the cerebellum, receiving input from muscle spindles, Golgi tendon organs, and pressure receptors. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Where is the first-order neuron of the posterior (dorsal) spinocerebellar tract found, and how does it ascend to the cerebellum?","choices":["Found from C8 to S3; ascends ipsilaterally to the inferior cerebellar peduncle and cerebellar vermis cortex","Found only within the lumbar region (L1-L5); descends caudally back into the lower cord","Found from C3 to T7; ascends via the fasciculus cuneatus to the accessory cuneate nucleus","Found from L1 to S2; decussates and ascends via the superior cerebellar peduncle"],"answer":0,"exp":"The first-order neuron of the posterior spinocerebellar tract is found from C8 to S3, ascending ipsilaterally to the inferior cerebellar peduncle and on to the cortex of the cerebellar vermis. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What sensory territory does the great auricular nerve cover?","choices":["Skin over the parotid gland, posterior aspect of the ear, and skin of the neck","Skin of the neck and shoulder only, sparing the ear and parotid region","Skin of the anterior triangle of the neck only, sparing the ear region","Motor innervation to the rhomboid muscles and levator scapulae"],"answer":0,"exp":"The great auricular nerve arises from C2 and C3, crosses anteriorly to the sternocleidomastoid, and travels toward the parotid gland; it receives sensory information from the skin over the gland, the posterior aspect of the ear, and skin of the neck. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What spinal nerve levels form the brachial plexus, and what does it innervate?","choices":["T12-L4; innervates the pelvic girdle and anterior thigh","L4-S4; innervates the pelvic girdle and lower limb muscles","C5-T1; innervates the muscles of the pectoral girdles and upper limbs","C1-C5; innervates the muscles of the neck and diaphragm"],"answer":2,"exp":"The brachial plexus (C5-T1) innervates the muscles of the pectoral girdles and upper limbs. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What is the correct organizational sequence of the brachial plexus, from the spinal cord outward?","choices":["Nerves → cords (posterior/lateral/medial) → divisions (anterior/posterior) → trunks (superior/middle/inferior) → roots (ventral rami)","Ventral rami (roots) → trunks (superior/middle/inferior) → divisions (anterior/posterior) → cords (posterior/lateral/medial) → nerves","Divisions (anterior/posterior) → cords (posterior/lateral/medial) → roots (ventral rami) → trunks (superior/middle/inferior) → nerves","Trunks (superior/middle/inferior) → roots (ventral rami) → nerves → cords (posterior/lateral/medial) → divisions (anterior/posterior)"],"answer":1,"exp":"The brachial plexus is organized, from the spinal cord outward, as ventral rami (roots), then trunks (superior, middle, inferior), then divisions (anterior and posterior), then cords (posterior, lateral, medial), and finally nerves. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which four nerves are given as examples of nerves formed by the brachial plexus?","choices":["Phrenic, ansa cervicalis, lesser occipital, and great auricular nerves","Femoral, obturator, sciatic, and pudendal nerves","Radial, ulnar, median, and musculocutaneous nerves","Iliohypogastric, ilio-inguinal, genitofemoral, and lateral femoral cutaneous nerves"],"answer":2,"exp":"The brachial plexus forms nerves including the radial, ulnar, median, and musculocutaneous nerves. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What spinal levels make up the lumbar plexus and sacral plexus respectively, and what does the combined lumbosacral plexus innervate?","choices":["Lumbar plexus (T12-L4) and sacral plexus (L4-S4); together innervate the muscles of the pelvic girdle and lower limbs","Only a single combined plexus exists, spanning T12-S4, with no separate lumbar or sacral levels","Lumbar plexus (C5-T1) and sacral plexus (C1-C5); together innervate the upper limb and shoulder","Lumbar plexus (L4-S4) and sacral plexus (T12-L4); together innervate the neck and diaphragm"],"answer":0,"exp":"The lumbar plexus (T12-L4) and sacral plexus (L4-S4) together make up the lumbosacral plexus, which innervates the muscles of the pelvic girdle and lower limbs. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What do the iliohypogastric and ilio-inguinal nerves innervate?","choices":["The gracilis, obturator externus, and adductor magnus muscles","The quadriceps femoris, sartorius, and iliopsoas muscles","The external oblique, internal oblique, and transverse abdominis","The gluteus medius, gluteus minimus, and tensor fasciae latae"],"answer":2,"exp":"The iliohypogastric nerve and ilio-inguinal nerve both innervate the external oblique, internal oblique, and transverse abdominis. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What does the femoral nerve innervate?","choices":["External and internal oblique, and transverse abdominis","Gluteus maximus","Quadriceps, sartorius, pectineus, and iliopsoas","Gracilis, obturator externus, and adductor magnus"],"answer":2,"exp":"The femoral nerve innervates the quadriceps, sartorius, pectineus, and iliopsoas. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What does the obturator nerve innervate?","choices":["Semimembranosus, semitendinosus, and adductor magnus","Gracilis, obturator externus, and adductor magnus","Quadriceps, sartorius, and iliopsoas","Gluteus minimus, gluteus medius, and tensor fasciae latae"],"answer":1,"exp":"The obturator nerve innervates the gracilis, obturator externus, and adductor magnus. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What sensory territory does the genitofemoral nerve cover?","choices":["Skin of the anteromedial surface of the thigh and external genitals","Skin of the neck and shoulder, per the supraclavicular nerve territory","Skin of the anterior, lateral, and posterior thigh, excluding genitals","Skin of the perineum, posterior thigh, and posterior leg"],"answer":0,"exp":"The genitofemoral nerve provides sensory information from the skin of the anteromedial surface of the thigh and the external genitals. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What does the superior gluteal nerve innervate, versus the inferior gluteal nerve?","choices":["Superior gluteal: piriformis and obturator internus; Inferior gluteal: quadratus femoris","Superior gluteal: gluteus maximus and piriformis; Inferior gluteal: gluteus medius and minimus","Superior gluteal: gluteus minimus, gluteus medius, and tensor fasciae latae; Inferior gluteal: gluteus maximus","Superior gluteal: gluteus maximus only; Inferior gluteal: gluteus minimus, medius, and tensor fasciae latae"],"answer":2,"exp":"The superior gluteal nerve innervates the gluteus minimus, gluteus medius, and tensor fasciae latae, while the inferior gluteal nerve innervates the gluteus maximus. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 19"}]};
